findings.description:
All 25 cases are v2-shaped (numeric v, clientId presence). The corpus has no v3 cases for:
- hello, stamped put/del, or loc with vs;
- chat, chat-key, leave, op-ack/nack;
- the snapshot-begin/snapshot/snapshot-end fence;
- duplicate snapshot ids, non-canonical base64url, counters above 2^63, deep nesting.

Neither consumer test runs frames through the production dispatcher:
- Android re-implements the version check with kotlinx and never calls SyncManager (SyncMaliciousFrameTest.kt:57-81).
- iOS only calls JSONSerialization and strictJSONInteger (SyncMaliciousFrameTests.swift:38-68).

As a result, none of S4-01, S4-03, S4-06 or S4-07 would be caught by the shared hostile-input corpus.

Android: isSendDue only needs a newer fix and 5 s elapsed (UnitSyncPresenceCadence.kt:19-47, 103). GPS runs at 1 Hz (LocationService.kt:82-88) and a 5 s loop drives sending (SyncManager.kt:3024-3036).

iOS: UnitSyncPresenceCadence.isDue/canBroadcast (SyncManager.swift:572-640), with best-accuracy updates and no distance filter plus a 5 s timer (2860-2871).

Neither platform has a movement, heading or speed threshold. Receivers keep a marker for 45 s (PresencePeer.kt:83) and sweep every 30 s, so a 20 s heartbeat would keep stationary markers fresh.

A stationary device therefore sends 720 frames/h where ~180 would do. Each frame costs:
- ~1.5 KB uplink;
- (N−1) × 1.5 KB of relay egress;
- 1 relay storage row (S5-05);
- N−1 full replay-state rewrites at the receivers (S5-01);
- 3 Ed25519 signs, and 3(N−1) verifies.

findings.suggested_fix:
Send immediately on:
- movement > max(5 m, accuracy/2);
- course change > 15°;
- speed change > 1 m/s;
- config change.

Otherwise send a heartbeat every 20 s, below the 45 s retention. Keep the current rate while moving.

No wire change, so old receivers are unaffected. Savings: ~75% of presence traffic, relay rows and receiver work for stationary units.

verdicts.reasoning:
Confirmed from sendPresenceV3 (SyncManager.kt:3209-3287). The envelope carries:
- the flat payload fields (putFlatFields) AND the canonical base64 'p';
- the inner pub;
- three separate Ed25519 signatures (loc, accuracy, retention), each with its own base64 payload.

The whole envelope is then AES-GCM sealed and base64'd, and the outer frame repeats by/pub/sd/vs. The receiver verifies all three signatures (applyPresenceV3 2828-2865).

The ~1.4-1.5 KB size is consistent with the field inventory. The reductions need a fleet minimum-version gate or a new pv, which the auditor correctly flags. This is a bandwidth and CPU inefficiency with no functional defect: medium.
